1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,694
Can you show me Jobs and CronJobs?

2
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:11,874
A Job runs a task until it succeeds, then stops:

3
00:00:11,874 --> 00:00:15,060
a database migration, a report, an import.

4
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:17,943
A CronJob creates a Job on a schedule:

5
00:00:17,943 --> 00:00:21,583
every night at 2:00, every minute, every Monday.

6
00:00:21,583 --> 00:00:22,494
The analogy:

7
00:00:22,494 --> 00:00:35,313
a Deployment is a shop that must stay open all day; a Job is a delivery that is done once the parcel arrives; a CronJob is the delivery round that happens every morning.

8
00:00:35,867 --> 00:00:38,550
A Deployment restarts its Pods forever:

9
00:00:38,550 --> 00:00:44,606
if your backup script finishes and exits, the Deployment would start it again and again.

10
00:00:44,606 --> 00:00:46,051
Some work has an end.

11
00:00:46,051 --> 00:00:51,075
A Job knows the difference between "finished successfully" and "crashed",

12
00:00:51,075 --> 00:00:58,231
retries failures a limited number of times, and keeps the result (the Pod and its logs) for you to read.

13
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:02,386
And how does it actually work?

14
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:08,803
The Job controller creates Pods from the Job's template and watches how they end:

15
00:01:08,803 --> 00:01:13,078
exit code 0 → one completion; when completions are reached,

16
00:01:13,078 --> 00:01:18,077
the Job is Complete; non-zero exit → a failure; a new Pod is created,

17
00:01:18,077 --> 00:01:22,063
up to backoffLimit retries, with a growing delay (10 s,

18
00:01:22,063 --> 00:01:27,859
20 s, 40 s…); after that the Job is Failed with the reason BackoffLimitExceeded.

19
00:01:27,859 --> 00:01:38,294
restartPolicy must be Never (a failed Pod is replaced by a new one) or OnFailure (the container is restarted inside the same Pod); never Always,

20
00:01:38,294 --> 00:01:40,540
which is for long-running Pods.

21
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:49,162
The CronJob controller checks the schedule every few seconds and creates a Job (named CRONJOB-<number>) when it is due.

22
00:01:49,162 --> 00:01:53,003
Old Jobs are deleted according to the history limits.

23
00:01:54,767 --> 00:01:54,941
1.

24
00:01:54,941 --> 00:01:57,386
A namespace for this lesson:

25
00:01:57,933 --> 00:01:58,074
2.

26
00:01:58,074 --> 00:02:01,458
Run the countdown Job and wait for it to finish:

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:02,149
3.

28
00:02:02,149 --> 00:02:03,569
Look at the result:

29
00:02:03,569 --> 00:02:03,719
4.

30
00:02:03,719 --> 00:02:08,652
Start the CronJob, and trigger one run by hand (--from=cronjob/...

31
00:02:08,652 --> 00:02:16,350
creates a Job from its template right away, useful to test a CronJob without waiting for its schedule):

32
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:17,049
4.

33
00:02:17,049 --> 00:02:21,978
Start the CronJob, and trigger one run by hand (--from=cronjob/...

34
00:02:21,978 --> 00:02:29,669
creates a Job from its template right away, useful to test a CronJob without waiting for its schedule):

35
00:02:31,433 --> 00:02:34,343
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

36
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:39,292
The Pods of a Job stay after it finishes (status Completed),

37
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:43,684
so you can read their logs; each carries the label job-name:

38
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:47,157
Let me try something.

39
00:02:47,157 --> 00:02:51,098
An import task reads a file that does not exist:

40
00:02:51,633 --> 00:02:53,715
What should happen?

41
00:02:53,715 --> 00:02:55,139
Complete 1/1.

42
00:02:55,139 --> 00:02:56,673
What happened?

43
00:02:56,673 --> 00:03:00,618
Failed, reason BackoffLimitExceeded.

44
00:03:02,367 --> 00:03:04,817
A failed Job does not start again:

45
00:03:04,817 --> 00:03:08,420
fix the cause, delete the Job and create it again.

46
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:16,347
Here the import should read a file the Pod has (in a real Job you would mount it from a ConfigMap or a volume,

47
00:03:16,347 --> 00:03:17,716
lessons 11 and 13):

48
00:03:19,467 --> 00:03:20,180
Got it.

49
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:22,931
What do I need to remember?

50
00:03:23,467 --> 00:03:31,546
Job = run to completion, retry failures up to backoffLimit; CronJob = a Job on a cron schedule.

51
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:36,099
completions and parallelism split work across Pods.

52
00:03:36,633 --> 00:03:40,009
A failed Job's Pods keep their logs:

53
00:03:40,009 --> 00:03:42,823
kubectl logs -l job-name=NAME.

54
00:03:44,567 --> 00:03:47,550
I keep hearing about DaemonSets.

55
00:03:47,550 --> 00:03:49,321
What is it, really?

56
00:03:49,867 --> 00:03:54,364
A DaemonSet runs one Pod on every suitable node of the cluster.

57
00:03:54,364 --> 00:04:00,574
Add a node, and it gets its Pod automatically; remove a node, and its Pod goes with it.

58
00:04:00,574 --> 00:04:01,431
The analogy:

59
00:04:01,431 --> 00:04:09,426
a Deployment is "three cashiers, wherever there is room"; a DaemonSet is "one security guard in every building",

60
00:04:09,426 --> 00:04:11,781
however many buildings there are.

61
00:04:12,333 --> 00:04:15,937
Some work belongs to the node, not to the application:

62
00:04:15,937 --> 00:04:21,609
collecting the node's log files, measuring its CPU and disk, configuring its network.

63
00:04:21,609 --> 00:04:24,545
That work must happen exactly once per node.

64
00:04:24,545 --> 00:04:34,488
With a Deployment you would have to keep replicas equal to the number of nodes and hope the scheduler spreads them evenly; a DaemonSet guarantees it.

65
00:04:36,233 --> 00:04:38,653
And how does it actually work?

66
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:42,255
The DaemonSet controller looks at every node,

67
00:04:42,255 --> 00:04:48,431
checks whether the node is suitable (it matches the nodeSelector, its taints are tolerated:

68
00:04:48,431 --> 00:04:53,251
lesson 16), and makes sure exactly one Pod of the DaemonSet runs there.

69
00:04:53,251 --> 00:04:55,083
There is no replicas field:

70
00:04:55,083 --> 00:04:58,545
the number of Pods is the number of suitable nodes.

71
00:04:58,545 --> 00:05:01,803
A node agent often needs to see the node itself,

72
00:05:01,803 --> 00:05:07,505
so it mounts a folder of the node with a hostPath volume (lesson 13 covers volumes):

73
00:05:07,505 --> 00:05:12,324
here, read-only, the folder where the node keeps the logs of every Pod.

74
00:05:14,067 --> 00:05:14,239
1.

75
00:05:14,239 --> 00:05:16,826
A namespace and the DaemonSet:

76
00:05:17,367 --> 00:05:17,591
2.

77
00:05:17,591 --> 00:05:19,496
One Pod per node:

78
00:05:21,233 --> 00:05:24,143
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

79
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:28,849
Where the Pod runs, and what the agent sees on its node:

80
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:32,451
Let me try something.

81
00:05:32,451 --> 00:05:37,474
A second agent, meant only for nodes labelled lab=agents:

82
00:05:38,033 --> 00:05:39,880
What should happen?

83
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:41,726
One disk-agent Pod.

84
00:05:41,726 --> 00:05:43,086
What happened?

85
00:05:43,086 --> 00:05:44,058
DESIRED 0:

86
00:05:44,058 --> 00:05:47,848
the DaemonSet does not even want a Pod.

87
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:57,004
Either remove the nodeSelector, or give the node the label (the usual way to say "this node is for this kind of agent"):

88
00:05:58,767 --> 00:05:59,480
Got it.

89
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:02,231
What do I need to remember?

90
00:06:02,767 --> 00:06:08,351
DaemonSet = exactly one Pod on every suitable node; no replicas.

91
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:13,709
"Suitable" = matches the nodeSelector and tolerates the node's taints.

92
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:16,820
DESIRED 0 is not an error:

93
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:18,391
no node matches.
